ion/math-academy-id "q-9643" :question/problem "Find the area bounded by the polar curve $r=3{e}^{2\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=3{e}^{2\\theta }$, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{(3{e}^{2\\theta })}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4\\theta }\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}({e}^{4\\pi }-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}({e}^{4\\pi }-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-9643/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a62e8412-1733-577c-bd60-25395e6fa908"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-9643"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-352338/field-1/501b808a8d98c263f48baefa27f429e00eadcab32400365727317227883af51d" :answer/id #uuid "bcfd0e40-b327-505a-a3bf-67837f9b1f67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8e^2}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352338/field-1/82792c3e7b855e17867144b5788505ae801c8389068d5f45b90b8b4704637903" :answer/id #uuid "bf4e0c89-ed9b-5492-b94e-37cca1f24084" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4e^2}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352338/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "78cffae8-2374-54b1-9142-284c31b23b6f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-352338/field-1/501b808a8d98c263f48baefa27f429e00eadcab32400365727317227883af51d" :answer-field/choices ["q-352338/field-1/501b808a8d98c263f48baefa27f429e00eadcab32400365727317227883af51d" "q-352338/field-1/82792c3e7b855e17867144b5788505ae801c8389068d5f45b90b8b4704637903"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-352338" :question/id #uuid "9bc37aca-125c-56a1-af2c-6a1af9e536d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-352338" :question/problem "Find the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r=2{e}^{1-\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2{e}^{1-\\theta }$, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}(2{e}^{1-\\theta })^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}4{e}^{2-2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{e}^{-2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}(-\\frac{{e}^{-2\\theta }}{2}){|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{2}({e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =-{e}^{2}(\\frac{1}{9}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{8{e}^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-352338/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a62e8412-1733-577c-bd60-25395e6fa908"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-352338"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-14884/selection/6d59706c4af51a6024a0ff25909ef3a308dcde0a24e2b8abda73c79d0276e914" :answer/id #uuid "718da9d6-2b7d-5651-9adb-e243e800e73b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta ))^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14884/selection/727061a492955647e07c47220278e139b3a56cf1b5e9caaf9da4633fbb260a83" :answer/id #uuid "0bd30146-a432-589f-a44b-73ed14899f70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta ))^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14884/selection/8be27aa10fa9b5a3e45b37e5b784babb4f3545b1618e07a2c6e269dabbc29f89" :answer/id #uuid "ff1dadc8-edeb-5431-99b6-2a871eed1a9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta ))^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14884/selection/263008e10cc63a1793f85f82eaaad9bcd0564030f8e1c6f539a4df470c7ef65e" :answer/id #uuid "1dc9dc3b-e2d2-5b3a-9013-393bc9646d89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta ))^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14884/selection/ae76541e699398e5bc7e71201cf5193f698a4589bde92d050d89b17a05cd3f78" :answer/id #uuid "4f5e01db-de73-59e8-b804-461667389814" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta ))^{2}\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-14884/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "03438a82-9691-5eca-92b6-df9227c79278" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-14884/selection/727061a492955647e07c47220278e139b3a56cf1b5e9caaf9da4633fbb260a83" :answer-field/choices ["q-14884/selection/6d59706c4af51a6024a0ff25909ef3a308dcde0a24e2b8abda73c79d0276e914" "q-14884/selection/727061a492955647e07c47220278e139b3a56cf1b5e9caaf9da4633fbb260a83" "q-14884/selection/8be27aa10fa9b5a3e45b37e5b784babb4f3545b1618e07a2c6e269dabbc29f89" "q-14884/selection/263008e10cc63a1793f85f82eaaad9bcd0564030f8e1c6f539a4df470c7ef65e" "q-14884/selection/ae76541e699398e5bc7e71201cf5193f698a4589bde92d050d89b17a05cd3f78"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-14884" :question/id #uuid "43a0d0b4-b0d6-5a8a-afea-fad5ffecb862" :question/math-academy-id "q-14884" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the total area enclosed by graph of the polar curve $r=2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )$, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta ))^{2}\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-14884/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a62e8412-1733-577c-bd60-25395e6fa908"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-14884"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-352330/field-1/0d31f583418270900c0c8eaaf1e6966f33bf5d987df13daf6cd09edefb0d5fa3" :answer/id #uuid "03344200-7948-5019-9dfd-49d98748cd46" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi^3}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352330/field-1/f06946c3bb2df687ce7852df0a992eb9ecb2d125283775f5fc339cce7e0a9447" :answer/id #uuid "89d40137-edc5-5668-a944-6e0d03ddfc7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi^3}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352330/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "acf766d9-60e8-506e-b773-5ec36fa788a1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-352330/field-1/0d31f583418270900c0c8eaaf1e6966f33bf5d987df13daf6cd09edefb0d5fa3" :answer-field/choices ["q-352330/field-1/0d31f583418270900c0c8eaaf1e6966f33bf5d987df13daf6cd09edefb0d5fa3" "q-352330/field-1/f06946c3bb2df687ce7852df0a992eb9ecb2d125283775f5fc339cce7e0a9447"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-352330" :question/id #uuid "0aa3c96c-3a12-54be-a246-58cadfe8bbe4" :question/math-academy-id "q-352330" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060520/assets/327f1f7a395616de463c054ec904812dcd422776522a262f956530d3aa9278eb.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r=2\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=2\\theta $, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\pi $, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }4{\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2(\\frac{{\\theta }^{3}}{3})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2(\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{3})-2(\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{\\pi }^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-352330/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a62e8412-1733-577c-bd60-25395e6fa908"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-352330"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-352337/field-1/597ed0bd2da4549760de998edfed7b395f8fb5d9545c6e6018236cefa6937daf" :answer/id #uuid "2e2f1d39-0c8b-55df-8de9-b97ef0fe5da0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3e^2}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352337/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a9674210-a3aa-534e-a1af-a681c694254a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-352337/field-1/597ed0bd2da4549760de998edfed7b395f8fb5d9545c6e6018236cefa6937daf" :answer-field/choices ["q-352337/field-1/597ed0bd2da4549760de998edfed7b395f8fb5d9545c6e6018236cefa6937daf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-352337" :question/id #uuid "8950de2c-8595-511b-8c63-6801ecf35ee7" :question/math-academy-id "q-352337" :question/problem "Find the area $A$ bounded by the polar curve $r={e}^{\\theta +1}$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )={e}^{\\theta +1}$, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{({e}^{\\theta +1})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{2\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}(\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2}){|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{4}({e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{4}(4-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-352337/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a62e8412-1733-577c-bd60-25395e6fa908"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-352337"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-632"] :question/problem "Find the area bounded by the polar curve $r = \\cos(3\\theta)$ and the lines $\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060520/assets/350a721c254dab3a8acb2ae54ca5be0bcde6ab036e6936939194c57ae55fe4ff.png)" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2(\\theta) \\, \\textrm{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta) = \\cos(3\\theta),$ $\\theta_1=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$ and $\\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\theta {|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}((\\frac{\\pi }{6}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}))+\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-633"] :question/problem "Find the area bounded by the polar curve $r = 1+2\\cos\\theta$ for $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060520/assets/6bfa0990b560bcb09f2a9eb0b9542296f2a8f1c196b6740702e81c59941d66c5.png)" :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2(\\theta) \\, \\textrm{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta) = 1+2\\cos(\\theta),$ $\\theta_1=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},$ and $\\theta_2=\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3},$ this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta ))^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}(1+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+2\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{2\\pi /3}^{4\\pi /3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -4\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -3\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6822"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060520/assets/d7b39805131dc562e233d49a84f69d30e80ca29cb9443012032d18fdb4512054.png)\n\nFind the area bounded by the polar curve $r = 2\\sin\\theta + 2\\cos\\theta$ for $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4},$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2(\\theta) \\, \\textrm{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta) = 2\\sin\\theta + 2\\cos\\theta,$ $\\theta_1=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ and $\\theta_2=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4},$ this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}(4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}(4+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2(\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}) \\\\  & =2\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi -2}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
